<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- net8.0 is the LTS most enterprise estates actually run; net10.0 is current. One
         compiler (global.json) builds both, and the unit suite runs against both, so the
         net8.0 asset is proven, not assumed. -->
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>

    <!-- Ship the XML doc file so the StandardAgent builder methods surface as IntelliSense
         tooltips for package consumers. CS1591 is suppressed on purpose: only the client /
         exposure layer carries doc comments (The Standard uses comments to say what the code
         can't), so the undocumented brokers and services below must not break the zero-warning
         bar. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Standard.Agents</PackageId>

    <!-- The Standard's release format is v1.2.3.4 — model . service/routine . fix/config
         . automated build. Deliberately NOT semver; the segments say what KIND of change
         happened, so a model change can never hide in the service segment.
         1.7.0.0: Evals and Hosting - the release the enterprise plan owed since 1.1,
         displaced five times by audits that outranked it - plus the three decisions the
         sweep left open, all ruled and closed. Standard.Agents.Evals pins QUALITY the way
         conformance pins contracts: task completion, groundedness, retrieval precision and
         recall, tool selection, refusal correctness and revision effectiveness, measured
         deterministically against golden cases through the real composition, thresholded,
         CI-gated, every metric proven able to fail, every report stamped with the framework
         version and the golden-set hash. Three ADVERSARIAL vectors certify what no suite
         did: a poisoned knowledge passage and a poisoned memory that FOOL the scripted
         Brain still cannot act outside the allow-list, and a fooled Brain cannot cross
         tenant scopes - each asserts the poison REACHED the Brain, so a pass means the
         attack was survived, not dodged. The Critical profile now REQUIRES all three and
         says "adversarial evaluation" again - words it lost when nothing verified them.
         Standard.Agents.Host is the exposure layer as The Standard defines one: controllers
         that are pure mapping over one IAgent dependency, a heartbeat, SSE streaming whose
         Response events equal the batched answer, and zero-config startup that stands and
         says what to configure. NOTHING was added to the builder for any of it. The three
         sweep decisions closed: a turn-capped run delivers prose about why and never
         records an answer it never gave (the one Skipped test now runs); Direction's
         standing orders travel as one PerimeterPolicy datum and a new tier rule bans naked
         delegates in every service constructor; and cancellation crosses the nesting seam -
         AgentRun carries the run's token and AgentTool hands it to the nested agent through
         the new stoppable IAgent.RunAsync(prompt, token). Also: the package multi-targets
         net8.0 and net10.0 with the suite green on both, the public API is declared in
         PublicAPI.Shipped.txt and guarded by the build, and the supply chain is watched
         between builds (Dependabot, CodeQL). New public routines and one new model, no
         contract removed, no field repurposed: a service/routine change, so segment 2
         advances, 3/4 reset.
         1.6.1.0: the docs said what the code does, and saying it caught a defect. The
         2026-08-23 sweep's most common finding was the silence — a true statement with an
         unstated boundary — and this pass states them where a reader decides: run-once is
         scoped to a run and an at-least-once trigger MUST be deduplicated at the trigger
         boundary (previously said only inside vector 33's JSON); nothing crosses the nesting
         seam — budget, principal, policy, approvals, ledger, sessions, grants, and the outer
         cancellation token — and what crosses back is the [did not complete] marker; .Risk()
         classifies and does not enforce; .Budget meters the Brain. False claims corrected:
         how-to described the retrieval matcher that 1.3 REPLACED, complete with a worked
         example that now behaves the opposite of what it showed; AgentOutcome's XML claimed a
         capped run produced prose (it carries the last tool result with Status Working);
         README said sixteen capabilities where the enforced matrix holds eighteen; and the
         Contract guardian - shipped and enforced since 1.5.0 - was documented in no
         consumer-facing doc at all. VERIFYING RATHER THAN TRANSCRIBING caught one more
         defect, sweep shape 3 at a new site: .Knowledge(minScore:) stored the floor and
         composition never passed it on, so a host raising the relevance floor changed
         nothing. One line fixes it; the class was swept (52 builder fields, zero other
         orphans) and the floor is documented with the ranking it floors. A behaviour fix and
         a documentation pass, no new public surface, no model change: a fix/config change,
         so segment 3 advances, 4 resets.
         1.6.0.0: the enforcement pass - the checks that could not fail were made to bite, and
         the one capability the matrix could not see got its missing modes. A vector that
         cannot fail reports coverage: the sweep proved redaction's vector passed with
         rehydration deleted and the scoped allow-list's passed with the scope check inverted,
         and traced wrong-reason passes through four more. Six vectors strengthened, each
         proven able to fail by applying the very mutation it exists to catch: 14 requires
         rehydration to run ({{EMAIL_0}} in the scripted reply), 34 pins WHICH act executed,
         08 pins that the Brain never saw the refused task, 26 declares a reversible tool that
         never ran and excludes it from the unwind, 28 asserts each prompt's outcome in order
         (held, then delivered - deletion of the approval control was previously invisible to
         it), and the new ask-approved-act-runs closes the Ask triangle with 35 and 37 so
         "deny everything under Ask, consult nobody" fails six vectors instead of zero. The
         harness now rejects unknown fields on Vector/Expectation/NativeReply (a typo'd key
         silently deleted the assertion it carried) and gains ResultsContain, per-prompt and
         in order. TierDisciplineTests gains the tool seam: Standard.Agents.Tools was scanned
         by no rule and held a live inversion - RememberTool kept IMemoryService, composed
         into ToolBroker, so a foundation transitively called a foundation through a broker.
         RememberTool now holds a Func like any host tool (the IMemoryService constructor
         stays as a converting [Obsolete] alias), and the new rule scans every framework
         tool's fields, generics included. Also encoded: every .Services namespace segment
         must be a checked tier; a capability that skips a mode must waive it with a reason
         beyond the bare N/A (a null-Local row with no waiver was silently unasserted).
         REDACTION JOINS THE MATRIX it was missing from entirely: .Redact(params
         RedactionRule[]) for the host's own patterns, .UseRedaction(IRedactionBroker) for a
         provider, .OnRedaction(redact, rehydrate) for rules no pattern expresses - wired
         through the same decorators, pinned behaviourally, sabotage-verified. New public
         routines and one new broker, no existing contract removed and no model change: a
         service/routine change, so segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset.
         1.5.3.0: every turn is measured, and a rejected draft still costs. On the text
         protocol only the FIRST turn was ever measured: Interpret carries the token counts
         across turns exactly as it carried Status, MeasuredAsync read the carried copy as
         "the provider already reported", and the loop re-billed turn 1's figure every turn —
         a 7-turn run whose prompt grows every turn was billed as seven times its smallest
         turn. The same shape as the eight-release hole 1.3.0 closed, introduced by the fix
         that closed it. FreshOfLastTurn now clears Status AND cost at both of Decision's
         doors — one clear for the whole "per-turn signal carried by the context copy" class,
         whose two members (0.12.0's Revising, 1.3.0's counts) were introduced by two
         different fixes and hid in the same copy. The guardian rejection paths carry the
         rejected draft's measured cost onto the Revising context, because a revision loop
         the budget cannot see is where a run burns tokens fastest.
         Two vectors close the certification hole that let this ship: every budget vector
         used a deliberately impossible bound, so an implementation that counted nothing and
         stopped immediately - or re-billed turn 1 forever - was certifiable.
         a-budget-counts-every-turn grows the prompt each turn and requires the bound to trip
         on real cumulative spend; a-generous-budget-lets-the-run-finish requires a run under
         a generous bound to complete, and was proven able to fail by mutating the check to
         "exhausted whenever bounded". Stated rather than silent: .Budget meters the Brain's
         calls only - guardians run small fixed verdicts, and .MaxTurns is the bound that
         covers everything - written into how-to 13 as a boundary instead of left to be
         discovered. Behaviour fixes on existing routines, no new public surface, no model
         change: a fix/config change, so segment 3 advances, 4 resets.
         1.5.2.0: one loop, two projections. The 2026-08-23 sweep found six controls enforced
         on one door and not the other — tool-output screening, the Contract, the revision
         reset, the approval hold's announcement, the Gate's route label (the mirror image:
         streamed only), and native tool calling, which THREW on the streamed door — and every
         one had been introduced by editing one loop and not its twin. Each was closed
         FAIL-first; then the class was closed: RunManagementService now has exactly ONE loop
         (a plain async method pumping a bounded channel), with RunAsync draining it and
         StreamPromptAsync yielding it, and the only named seam between the doors is which
         Brain protocol Decision drives. Decision's own two doors were likewise reduced to thin
         projections over shared verdict handling: entry normalization of a carried Revising
         (which also fixes the tool-call-after-rejection swallow on BOTH doors — the third exit
         of the 1.5.0 revision bug), gate refusal and routing, the Judge-then-Contract
         sequence, and the model-call narration, each written once.
         THE PUMP IS NOT AN ITERATOR, for a reason found the hard way: an async iterator's
         execution context does not survive a yield boundary, so the ambient AgentRun — the
         identity run-once keys, approval grants, compensation and record attribution hang off
         — silently reverted to null after the first streamed event. The OLD streamed loop had
         exactly that shape: those controls were quietly detached from the run identity between
         events, and nothing saw it because no streamed test exercised them. Unifying the loops
         surfaced it as twelve immediate batched-test failures, which is the one-loop argument
         demonstrated by the refactor that implemented it.
         Exception mapping is now a control on both doors at every tier that maps —
         RunManagement, Decision, Inference — via one MappedAndLoggedAsync per tier that
         rethrows through that tier's own TryCatch, and streamed wrappers that advance the
         enumeration inside the catch and yield outside it: the answer to "an iterator cannot
         wrap a yield" is that the failure points are the awaits. Two deliberate streamed
         alignments to the batched contract: a stopped run no longer writes Remember, and a
         turn-capped run whose unwind reported something no longer appends the session.
         LoopParityTests replaces enumerated parity with DERIVED parity: fourteen scenarios
         through both doors, requiring the answer, the tool executions and the entire
         decision-log trace to be identical — every control narrates itself, so a control
         added to one door only diverges without the test naming it. Sabotage-verified at the
         seam: the streamed door was made to skip screening and the suite went red on the trace.
         Behaviour fixes and an internal restructure on existing routines, no new public
         surface, no model change: a fix/config change, so segment 3 advances, 4 resets.
         1.5.1.0: the permission hotfix — four silent grants closed, none of them new rules.
         All four violate SPEC 1.3 §4.9 as it shipped in 1.5.0, which is why this is a fix
         segment and the spec is untouched: when a rule and the code disagree, the code changes.
         PERMISSIONMODE.DENY WAS NEVER CONSULTED. Declared, documented ("Denied. Nothing runs
         but what was named."), and compared against nothing — the only read of the mode checked
         Ask, so the strictest disposition behaved exactly like Open. The perimeter gains step
         1b: an act nothing named is refused under Deny after authorization and before its
         intent is recorded, told and non-terminal like a policy denial; an act RequireApproval
         names still travels to its authority. The class was swept, not the instance: every enum
         in src was checked for members with zero references outside their declaration, and
         Deny was the only one.
         ASK WITH NOBODY WIRED TO ANSWER WAS CONSENT. Ask promises what RequireApproval
         delivers - held, because waiting is not consent - but with no approver configured it
         routed to NotConfiguredApprovalBroker, which answered Approved unconditionally; and
         RequireApprovalBroker answered Approved for any tool NOT on its list, a branch only
         reachable under Ask. Both brokers now answer Pending wherever no human could have said
         yes. The class was swept: of every NotConfigured/rule broker default, these two were
         the only ones a switched-on control routed to for a grant.
         ONE APPROVAL COVERED EVERY LATER USE OF THE TOOL. A grant is keyed on the tool AND the
         scope, exactly - but ScopeOf defaults to empty, so for any tool that names no scope
         (every MCP tool among them) the key collapsed to the tool name and approving a $10
         transfer silently approved the $10,000 one. An act with no named scope now remembers
         nothing and is asked about each time; an identical repeat is already replayed by
         run-once before approval is reached. The class was swept: no other derived key in src
         collapses on an empty component.
         THE STREAMED LOOP NEVER SCREENED WHAT A TOOL HANDED BACK. ScreenedAsync had exactly one
         call site - the batched loop - so with .ScreenToolOutput() on, the injected result the
         batched path withholds sailed into the Brain whenever the caller streamed. The streamed
         loop now screens on the same terms, before the Tool event is yielded, and unwinds
         through screening failures as it does through Recall and Act. This one is deliberately
         an instance fix: the remaining batched/streamed asymmetries (contract, revision reset,
         AwaitingApproval event, route capture, native streaming, exception mapping) are a
         class the loop unification closes next, and an open injection path does not queue
         behind a refactor.
         Three new vectors - deny-covers-what-nothing-permitted, ask-with-no-authority-holds,
         a-grant-is-for-what-it-was-granted-for - each observed failing against 1.5.0 before
         the fix, each pinning the positive direction too, so an implementation that denies or
         holds everything fails them as surely as one that grants everything. All three join
         the Enterprise profile. Behaviour fixes on existing routines, no new public surface,
         no model change: a fix/config change, so segment 3 advances, 4 resets.
         1.5.0.0: what an answer must look like, what a sub-agent actually did, and what
         permission means.
         ContractService is a third guardian. The client returned a string and nothing else, so
         every consumer wiring an agent into a workflow wrote a parser - the thing this framework
         talked them out of doing everywhere else. A schema check asks whether an answer is the
         right SHAPE beside the Judge asking whether it is good ENOUGH, so it sits in the Guardian
         region rather than becoming a new concept, and a mismatch takes the Judge's revision path
         with the validation error as the reason. .Contract / .UseContract / .OnContract.
         Two defects surfaced while building it and are fixed here rather than filed.
         A REVISION THAT PASSED REVIEW NEVER DELIVERED. Interpret carries the incoming context
         forward and nothing reset Status, so a draft accepted on the second pass left Decision
         still marked Revising, the loop continued, and the run exhausted its turns handing back
         "I can't help with that at the moment" for an answer that had passed. Reachable with
         nothing but a Judge, present since the revision loop shipped in 1.2.0.0, and covered by
         no test because every test asserted permanent rejection and none asserted rejection then
         acceptance. The retry existed and could not succeed.
         A HELD SUB-AGENT READ EXACTLY LIKE AN ANSWER. IAgent returned only a string, so an outer
         agent could not tell a nested run that answered from one held on an approval - it
         received "'wire_transfer' is waiting for approval before it can run", took it as the
         tool's output, and could report work as done that a human had not permitted. IAgent gains
         RunAsync as a default interface member, reporting result AND status; AgentTool returns an
         answer plainly and marks everything else. RunManagementService returns an AgentOutcome
         with ProcessPromptAsync projecting the answer out of it, and StopAsync stops discarding
         the status it was handed.
         PERMISSION IS WHAT AND WHERE. The allow-list could name a tool and nothing else, so "may
         write files" could not be told from "may write files under /project". ITool declares its
         own Risk and names what it is about to touch through ScopeOf, because only the tool knows
         what its arguments mean; AgentEffect carries the scope to the policy, the authority and
         the audit record; and an allow-list entry may say where. RiskLevel.Sensitive was
         UNREACHABLE - risk and approval were one predicate over one collection, so a tool was
         Irreversible if it needed approval and Safe if it did not, and the middle level named a
         state the framework could not produce. .Permissions(PermissionMode.Ask) answers for the
         acts nothing named, which is the only case that matters when targets cannot be enumerated
         at composition, and a granted approval is remembered for the tool AND the scope it was
         granted for, for the life of the run.
         New public routines, one new model, and two new optional interface members with defaults
         that preserve existing behaviour. No contract removed and no field repurposed: a
         service/routine change, so segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset. SPEC.md settles at 1.3 in the
         same pass, stating that risk MUST NOT be derived from approval and that permission is
         what and where.
         1.4.0.0: the last deviation closed. DirectionCoordinationService held Decision's Gate to
         screen tool output - a coordination reaching a foundation two tiers below it, and a
         foundation belonging to a different nature. It satisfied every rule already written:
         three dependencies, so the 2-3 test passed, and no broker, so the broker test passed.
         Nothing about the count was wrong. What was wrong was the DIRECTION of the reach, and no
         test of counts or brokers was ever going to see it - which is the argument for writing
         adjacency down as a rule of its own rather than treating it as something the count
         implies. ShouldDependOnlyOnTheTierDirectlyBelowIt was written first and watched failing
         on exactly [IGateService] before anything moved.
         Screening is now the loop's: RunManagementService records how many observations exist
         before Direction acts and screens what Direction added before the next turn can read it,
         asking Decision for the verdict because Decision owns the guardians. Direction performs
         the act, Decision judges the text, and neither knows the other exists. It reads better
         there than it did in Direction - what may enter the context between turns is the loop's
         question, and the loop is the only place that sees both natures.
         Behaviour is unchanged: a refusal still replaces the observation rather than appending
         beside it, and still answers the stranded tool call so no provider sees a call without a
         matching tool message. The acceptance test covering it was sabotage-verified AFTER the
         move rather than before - refusal detection disabled, the test watched failing - because
         a move is exactly when a control can stop working while every test still passes.
         Internal only: the builder surface, every model and every vector are untouched, and
         .ScreenToolOutput() means what it always did. A service/routine change, so segment 2
         advances, 3/4 reset. No deviations remain and the diagram says so without an asterisk.
         1.3.0.0: budgets work on every protocol, and the architecture stopped lying about why.
         TierDisciplineTests enforced that no broker sits above the foundation tier and said
         nothing about counts - so the 2-3 rule, the rule the whole 1.2 realignment was carried
         out to honour, was the one rule nothing was checking, and InferenceOrchestrationService
         shipped holding a single foundation. A service with one dependency composes nothing; it
         is a layer and an exception hop for no work.
         What was missing was a foundation, and the gap was load-bearing. PromptTokens and
         CompletionTokens were only ever set on the native V1 path, where they were lifted out of
         the provider's usage object as it went past. On the text protocol nobody was asked, so
         every run added zero to its spend and .Budget(maxTokens:) / .Budget(maxCostUsd:) never
         tripped at all - while the Enterprise profile went on claiming budgets. V0 is not
         deprecated; it is the contract that works against any endpoint. .Budget's own
         documentation promised "measured against what providers reported, never an estimate",
         which was the defect written down as a guarantee.
         UsageService over IUsageBroker answers the question, and Inference holds Brain and Usage.
         The provider's report still wins wherever there is one - it is what the invoice is drawn
         from - and counting is the fallback, covering the text protocol batched, the text
         protocol streamed, and any V1 endpoint that omits its usage object. AgentUsage and
         AgentContext both carry whether a number was reported or counted, because a bound
         enforced on an estimate and a bound reconciled against a bill are different claims.
         Counting is always on and costs nothing to leave on; BLOCKING is what has to be asked
         for, so an agent given no budget stays wide open. .Usage / .UseUsage / .OnUsage complete
         the triad, and the capability matrix now holds Usage to it.
         The shape is 1-3-6-14: ten foundations always present, four optional. Decision grew in
         the enterprise program like the other two natures did - the earlier claim that it had
         gained nothing, which was the whole basis for thinking it did not deserve regions, was
         wrong, and wrong because the growth had been hidden inside a broker.
         New public routines and one new optional model field, no existing field repurposed and
         no contract removed: a service/routine change, so segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset.
         1.2.0.0: the architecture put back on The Standard. Auditing the diagram against the
         code found five brokers in tiers where a broker is not allowed - all of them added by
         the enterprise program, none of them caught, because nothing was checking. The cost was
         not stylistic: a tier that calls a broker directly skips the foundation that would have
         given it validation and exception mapping, so a full disk in the effect ledger surfaced
         as a raw IOException blamed on Direction. Policy, Approval, EffectLedger and Session are
         now foundations of their own; redaction and resilience became DECORATING brokers, wrapped
         around another broker at composition, which makes "every model call is redacted" true by
         construction rather than by each service remembering. That left six foundations under
         Direction, which breaks the 2-3 rule and means the nature was holding two concepts - so
         each nature splits into two concept-named regions (Retrieval/Recollection,
         Inference/Guardian, Perimeter/Execution) with a coordination over them, and the loop
         moved up to RunManagementService. TierDisciplineTests now enforces all of it, so the
         next erosion fails the build instead of waiting for an audit. The 1-3-9 is untouched:
         the four new foundations are OPTIONAL capabilities, so drop them and the regions and the
         coordination tier collapse back to it - the full shape is 1-3-6-13, the same picture at a
         second magnification. Entirely internal: the client contract, every model and every
         conformance vector are unchanged, so this is a service/routine change: segment 2
         advances, 3/4 reset.
         1.1.0.0: two defects found by auditing 1.0 against its own claims, and the documentation
         brought up to what shipped. Identity now reaches the AUTHORIZATION decision and not only
         the record of it: AgentEffect.For was called with principal: null, hardcoded, while the
         principal resolver was wired to the logging broker alone - so the Enterprise profile
         claimed identity-aware authorization and delivered identity-aware reporting. It is
         resolved per act, since a singleton serves many callers. The streamed loop now enforces
         every control the batched one does - cancellation, budgets, sessions, compensation -
         because a control a caller can step around by changing method is not a control; it
         gained a sessionId overload, and cancellation and exhaustion arrive as Status rather
         than as an answer. Identity grew a shape: AgentPrincipal carries tenant, jurisdiction
         and delegation, added ALONGSIDE the principal identifier rather than replacing it, so
         nothing written against 1.0.0 breaks. A session now carries the effect it is waiting on,
         so a resuming process can show an authority the act rather than only the news that
         something waits. New public routines and new optional model fields, no existing field
         repurposed and no contract removed: a service/routine change, so segment 2 advances,
         3/4 reset. SPEC.md settles at 1.0 in the same pass, naming three mechanisms it had
         required results from without describing - run continuity across processes, native
         tool-call attribution, and the principal reaching the decision.
         1.0.0.0: the shape of an agent changed, which is why segment 1 moves — once, here, with
         every model change in the program batched into it rather than dribbled across eight
         releases. A run is no longer confined to one process. AgentSession carries the run that
         worked it, written at the START of the run rather than the end, because a crash means
         nothing at the end runs at all; a session that never delivered an answer is resumed with
         the interrupted run's identity, so the idempotency keys still match and an act that
         already went out is replayed rather than performed twice. FileEffectLedgerBroker makes
         run-once outlive the process that claimed it, the claim being an atomic file creation.
         An act that was HELD - denied, or waiting on an authority - now gives its claim back,
         which fixes an approval that could previously only ever be granted too late. Effects
         that cannot be made idempotent at all can be unwound: ITool.CompensateAsync declares how
         a tool is undone, .CompensateOnFailure() unwinds a failed run in reverse order over what
         it actually performed, and a tool that declares no way back is reported as an effect
         that stands rather than silently counted as undone. Native tool calls round-trip:
         a result comes back as a tool message naming the call that asked for it, which is the
         one thing the text protocol cannot express - and V0 is NOT deprecated, because it is the
         contract that works against any endpoint. Every one of the fifteen capabilities now
         answers Local, External and Custom, with the only omissions two documented
         impossibilities. All four readiness profiles certify, Critical included, across 29
         conformance vectors and 375 tests. New models (AgentSession.RunId, ToolExchange,
         PerformedEffect, CompensationOutcome) and a changed AgentContext: a model change, so
         segment 1 advances, 2/3/4 reset.
         0.18.0.0: the capability verbs now say what they mean. Every capability is reachable
         three ways — Local (in the box: point at a file, a folder, a rule), External (a provider
         package, passed through .UseX) and Custom (your own code, inline) — and the verbs are
         .X / .UseX / .OnX in that order. The three delegate-taking methods were named for the
         wrong mode: a delegate you write is Custom, not Local, so LocalBrain / LocalGate /
         LocalJudge are now OnBrain / OnGate / OnJudge. The old names remain as behavioral
         aliases marked [Obsolete] for a published deprecation window and are covered by a test
         that pins them to the new verbs, so no consumer breaks. New public routines on the
         client, the spec's core AgentContext model unchanged, so this is a service/routine
         change: segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset.
         0.14.0.0: skills that contradict each other no longer resolve silently. The Gate detects
         a direct contradiction across the active skill instructions (DetectConflictAsync, model-
         driven) and, instead of picking one, the agent asks the user which to follow
         (AgentStatus.AwaitingInput) — then learns the answer as a durable memory preference, so
         future identical conflicts auto-resolve without asking. The new surface is internal (a
         Remember field on AgentContext carries the learned choice out to the coordinator, which
         persists it through a Data RememberAsync write path); the client contract is unchanged,
         so this is a service/routine change: segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset.
         0.13.0.0: the streaming contract now matches the batch one. A prompt's answer reaches
         the caller's Response channel only once the Judge has settled it — the brain's draft
         streams as Thinking and a rejected draft never leaks into Response — so filtering a
         stream to Response equals what ProcessPromptAsync returns. The Recall→Think→Act turn
         cap, previously a hardcoded 7, is now configurable through .MaxTurns(n) on the client:
         the shared budget across tool calls and Judge revisions. A new public routine on the
         client (MaxTurns), the spec's core AgentContext model unchanged, so this is a
         service/routine change: segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset.
         0.12.0.0: the guardian review loop refuses gracefully instead of faulting. A Judge
         rejection is now a re-think signal (AgentStatus.Revising) rather than an empty direction
         routed into Act, which used to throw; the coordinator retries within the turn budget and,
         when the answer still cannot pass review, returns "I can't help with that at the moment."
         A behavioral fix on the existing loop, the public contract unchanged, so this is a
         service/routine change: segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset.
         0.11.0.0: configurable guardian conscience. .Constitution(path) prepends an ethical
         charter to BOTH guardian rubrics (Gate and Judge), above the built-in policy; and
         .Consumption(path) swaps a domain policy in for that built-in one. The framework-owned
         output contract is always kept below either, so a replacement policy can never break the
         guardian's wiring — the gate/judge prompts are now split into a replaceable policy and a
         fixed contract. Both inputs are optional and degrade to the built-in policy if absent.
         New public routines on the client (Constitution / Consumption), the spec's core
         AgentContext model unchanged, so this is a service/routine change: segment 2 advances,
         3/4 reset. Conformance now certifies the layering — a constitution binds both guardians,
         a consumption skill replaces the policy in both, and a gate refusal short-circuits the
         brain — and the package publishes to NuGet on a GitHub Release.
         0.10.0.0: a documentation and packaging pass — the README now carries a rendered
         architecture diagram and a "Create Your First AI Agent from Scratch" walkthrough
         video (both surface on the NuGet page), a cumulative how-to, and a swappable-backends
         guide; conformance vector 03 now asserts tool input, catching first-line parsing bugs.
         0.9.0.0: a simplest-start constructor (new StandardAgent(url, key, model)) and
         in-process guardians (LocalGate / LocalJudge) — so a single local model can back the
         brain, gate and judge with no HTTP — plus XML doc comments across the builder API that
         now ship in the package. New public routines on the client, the spec's core
         AgentContext model unchanged, so this is a service/routine change: segment 2 advances,
         3/4 reset. Still tracks SPEC.md v0.1 (draft): segment 1 goes to 1 when the spec settles.
         (0.8.0.0: memory and knowledge came alive — knowledge retrieval in Recall + a built-in
         'remember' tool.) -->
    <!-- 1.8.0.0 "Agent As Data": the whole configurable surface as one JSON document
         (FromJson / FromJsonFile, unknown keys refuse with the key named), telemetry as the
         fourth utility broker (BCL ActivitySource/Meter, GenAI semconv, all three verbs), and
         the Anthropic Messages API as a second in-box native wire shape (NativeBrainAnthropic).
         New public routines on the client and a new broker seam; the core models are unchanged,
         so segment 2 advances and 3/4 reset. Tracks SPEC.md v1.4, which names both additions. -->
    <!-- 1.9.0.0 "Plural Integrations": what the agent connects to accumulates, never replaces -
         many MCP servers behind one seam (tools/list discovery, name-owned routing,
         first-registered wins, per-server auth: none, API key, OAuth bearer, refresh delegate)
         and many skill sources concatenating in registration order, with "skills" and "mcp"
         arrays in the JSON document. New routines and a widened broker contract, core models
         unchanged: segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset. Tracks SPEC.md v1.5. -->
    <!-- 1.10.0.0 "The Fleet": other agents become a capability like every other. An agent
         registry reached three ways (.Agents folder of agent documents / .UseAgents broker /
         .OnAgents delegate), registered agents materializing as tools so the perimeter that
         governs acts governs handoffs, identity riding in the document (name/description), the
         fleet as data ("agents" key), grounded template-configurable handoffs, and a third
         reply verb TRANSFER: that ends the run with the specialist's answer verbatim - only
         when it actually answered. New routines and models, core contracts unchanged: segment
         2 advances, 3/4 reset. Tracks SPEC.md v1.6. -->
    <!-- 1.11.0.0 "Per-Request Inference": the seam an exposed agent was blocked on. A request
         (PromptRequest) carries its own sampling values, response schema, opaque provider
         passthrough, and caller-executed tools; precedence - configured, then request, then
         framework default - resolves once at the boundary into ResolvedInference, which is what
         rides the loop. Hard-configured always wins and is now distinguishable from defaulted
         (InferenceSettings went nullable, .Brain's knobs opt-in). One schema survives and seeds
         the wire AND the guardian; core-owned wire keys are stripped from the passthrough;
         caller tools are vocabulary, never capability - a call naming one ends the run
         AwaitingInput as the session's pending effect. Request-carrying overloads widen
         IGeneratorBroker/IGeneratorBrokerV1 additively (HonorsRequest, graceful degradation),
         both doors carry the resolution, and one composition serves concurrent heterogeneous
         requests. New routines and models, core contracts widened additively: segment 2
         advances, 3/4 reset. Tracks SPEC.md v1.7. -->
    <!-- 1.12.0.0 "The Stateless Caller": the first exposed consumer's two findings, closed.
         The pending caller call rides the run's outcome as well as the session
         (AgentOutcome.PendingEffect, AgentEffect.CallId - the model-minted id), because a
         stateless deployment has no session and the yield is the whole mechanism. And the
         request carries the caller-owned transcript (PromptRequest.History / ToolExchanges),
         rendered on both reply protocols exactly as a session's history would be - session
         wins when both exist. Parallel caller calls stay one-per-run, recorded as a ruling.
         New routines and additively-widened models: segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset. Tracks
         SPEC.md v1.8. -->
    <!-- 1.12.1.0 "The Wire Validates": the first production consumer found what no scripted
         broker could - RememberTool's Parameters was example JSON, not a JSON Schema, and a
         strict hosted provider (Azure OpenAI) rejects the WHOLE request over one bad tool
         schema, failing every native run the moment memory was composed in. The tool now ships
         a real schema, and GeneratorBrokerV1 degrades any non-object schema (salvaging a
         type-less one with properties) so one bad tool can never take down the turn again.
         Bug fix, no API change: segment 3 advances, 4 resets. -->
    <!-- 1.13.0.0 "The Agent Speaks": the run gains a voice. A fifth stream event kind,
         Narration - user-voiced progress prose, distinct from machine-voiced Status and the
         unvetted Thinking draft. The model narrates with a SAY: pre-line (peeled before the
         first-line rule; carried natively as GenerationResult.Narration), tools declare
         templates as the floor (ITool.NarrationStarting/Observed, {tool}/{payload} slots),
         and the loop - the single copy both doors share - screens every model-authored line
         through the Gate before voicing it: a refused narration is withheld everywhere and
         recorded as WITHHELD. The prose never enters the answer, observations, or history.
         New routines and additively-widened models: segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset. Tracks
         SPEC.md v1.9. -->
    <!-- 1.14.0.0 "The Streamed Outcome": the loop's third reading. RunStreamAsync yields every
         event live and its completion carries the same structured outcome RunAsync returns -
         status, result, the pending effect with its model-minted call id - because a caller
         should never choose between the answer's structure and the run's story (the first
         consumer, needing the pending call, ran batched and watched its narration land in one
         lump with the answer). The loop always computed the outcome on the streamed door;
         ProcessPromptStreamAsync discarded it, and this release stops discarding it.
         AgentRunStream is the decision tier's stream-plus-result shape one tier up; the IAgent
         default adapts StreamPromptAsync with RunAsync's documented assumption. Spec-first:
         tracks SPEC.md v1.10 (SS4.14). New routines and models: segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset. -->
    <!-- 1.15.0.0 "Selection": what an agent carries and what a run is offered are different
         things. OnSelectTools names, per run, the subset of described tools the run is offered
         - the text catalog and the native tool list render only that subset - so a greeting is
         offered nothing and a twenty-server agent never puts twenty catalogs in front of
         "Hello" (the motivating production finding: a greeting ran a web search six times the
         day live narration shipped). Selection narrows the offering, never the perimeter: an
         unselected tool stays callable-by-name under the same governance; caller tools are
         never selected away; unknown names are ignored and the trace records the truth of the
         offering; identical on all three doors. ToolSelector carries the delegate per the tier
         discipline; AgentRun.OfferedTools carries the offering per run. Spec-first: tracks
         SPEC.md v1.11 (SS4.15). New routines and models: segment 2 advances, 3/4 reset. -->
